2. Now consider the version in ThreadsAccountExperimentsMany.java
(in the directory exercises09).
Consider these four lines of the transfer:
Account min = accounts[Math.min(source.id, target.id)];
Account max = accounts[Math.max(source.id, target.id)];
synchronized(min) {
    synchronized(max) {
        // transaction logic
    }
}

Account s = accounts[source.id];
Account t = accounts[target.id];
synchronized(s) {
    synchronized(t) {
        // transaction logic
    }
}
Explain why the calculation of min and max are necessary?
Run the program with both versions of the code
shown above and explain the results of doing this.

Lets assume we have the second version and encounters this:
T1: 5 -> 7 (100kr)
T2: 7 -> 5 (100kr)

Here T1 will lock 5, and T2 will lock 7.
But now T1 waits forever trying to lock 7, and T2 waits forever trying to lock 5.
This is a deadlock!

If it is:
T1: 5 -> 7 (100kr)
T2: 5 <- 7 (100kr)
T1 will lock 5.
T2 will try to lock 5, and wait.
T1 will lock 7. Make the transaction and release both locks.
T2 will lock 5 and then 7, and make the transaction.
Therefore we have no deadlock!
So by always locking the account with the smaller ID (min)
first and the larger ID (max) second, the code guarantees that all
threads acquire locks in a consistent order. This avoids Deadlock:


